      "notes": "All four claims are well-supported by their cited evidence. E1 and E2 are measurable baselines drawn from multi-source evidence including parliamentary library sources. The 87% reduction figure in E2 is a specific quantitative claim; it is cited from a multi-source evidence unit including commonslibrary.parliament.uk, which is an acceptable institutional anchor. E5 correctly describes the declaration-of-incompatibility mechanism as carrying political rather than legal force. The sole projected claim (E8) accurately represents a debate rather than asserting an outcome. Tier integrity holds throughout. Magnitude ('minor') and time_horizon ('long-term') are appropriate: the policy is defensive/preservationist with no new rights expansion, and the effect is contingent on a repeal threat materialising. The rationale correctly identifies the counterfactual as a plausible repeal scenario, supported by E13 and E8 referencing the government-commissioned review and legislative proposals. No party identity or motive-attribution language is present. No false balance: direction is 'improves' and the claims support that consistently. Lever leakage is absent \u2014 the verdict correctly frames HRA retention through its effect on O9 fundamentals (access to justice, due-process checks, equal-treatment protections) rather than scoring the policy as a lever in its own right. One minor observation: several sources are classified as 'media' but are advocacy organisations (Liberty, Amnesty, BIHR, HRW); this is consistent across the verdict and no single advocacy source is the sole basis for any claim, so no asymmetric handling issue arises."
    },
